(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the role of oral corticosteroids in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Long-term after any admission","Acute exacerbations only","Daily maintenance for group B","First-line for group A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral steroids are for acute exacerbations only in this disease, which is why they are missing from the list of common chronic therapies. Exacerbations are treated with antibiotics, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and oxygen. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 49"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has spirometry for suspected asthma. Before a bronchodilator her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 61% predicted with a ratio of 55%; after albuterol it is 94% predicted, a 54% improvement. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Poor patient effort","Normal spirometry","Fixed airflow obstruction","Reversible airflow obstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A reduced 1-second volume and a reduced ratio before the bronchodilator show airflow limitation, and a 54% improvement after albuterol, far above the 12% threshold, shows reversibility. A positive bronchodilator response strongly supports asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has asthma symptoms 3 days a week, wakes at night 3 times a month, and uses albuterol 3 days a week but not daily. She has minor limitation of activity. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 72% predicted with a ratio reduced by 4%. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms, night waking, rescue use and minor limitation fit mild persistent, but a 1-second volume between 60% and 80% predicted (72%) is a moderate persistent feature, and severity follows the most severe category in which any feature occurs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is admitted with coronavirus disease 2019. His oxygen saturation is 90% on room air, and he needs supplemental oxygen. Which medication is the core treatment?","choices":["Molnupiravir","Dexamethasone","Azithromycin","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized adults with hypoxemia receive dexamethasone, usually for up to 10 days or until discharge, with remdesivir considered by timing and respiratory support. Oxygenation and severity, not simply a positive test or elapsed time, decide the pathway, and his saturation of 90% puts him on it. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 121, 122, 126"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman being treated for community-acquired pneumonia becomes newly confused. Her heart rate is 118/min, blood pressure 84/50 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 87% on room air, and her urine output has fallen. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Septicemia","Empyema","Pleurisy","Lung abscess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When pneumonia becomes systemic it progresses toward bacteremia or septicemia: tachycardia with hypotension, hypoxia, confusion or altered mental status, and decreased urine output or kidney function. She has every one of those signs, which changes her site of care and urgency of support. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 28, 53"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease uses his albuterol inhaler 10 to 12 times a day and has noticed palpitations and anxiety. Which explanation should guide counseling?","choices":["Excess albuterol carries cardiac risks","Albuterol use is harmless at any amount","He should stop albuterol entirely","His symptoms come from the antimuscarinic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should have albuterol, but it should not be used excessively because of toxicity and the risk of cardiac and psychiatric side effects. His heavy use also signals that his maintenance therapy and group need review. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a mild exacerbation with increased breathlessness. She is alert, her vital signs are stable, she has no new signs, and her husband is at home with her. Which setting of care is most appropriate?","choices":["Hospital ward admission","Intensive care admission","Noninvasive ventilation","Outpatient management"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. More than 80% of exacerbations can be managed as outpatients. She meets no admission criterion: no marked symptoms despite treatment, no need for noninvasive ventilation, no new signs or arrhythmia, and support at home. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"What are amphoric breath sounds, heard with a lung abscess?","choices":["Grating pleural rub","High-pitched expiratory wheeze","Rhonchi that clear with cough","Low-pitched with high overtones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amphoric breath sounds are low-pitched with some high-pitched overtones as air moves across a large open cavity, like breathing across an open jar. A lung abscess may also give dullness, whispered pectoriloquy and bronchophony. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with long-standing heart disease has 3 days of worsening shortness of breath, swelling of both legs, and new wheezing. He has no history of asthma or allergies, and his wheeze began as his heart failure decompensated. Which explanation for his wheezing is most likely?","choices":["Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Cardiac asthma","Late-onset intrinsic asthma","Extrinsic allergic asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac asthma is wheezing caused by decompensated heart failure. A 74-year-old with heart disease, leg swelling and wheeze that began with the decompensation, and no asthma or atopy, fits it; the heart failure, not the airway, needs treatment. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with cirrhosis and an albumin of 1.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0 g/dL) has a metabolic acidosis, a chloride of 110 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L) and an anion gap of 11 (normal 9 to 15). Which finding most limits how much the normal gap can reassure you?","choices":["Her high chloride","Her metabolic acidosis itself","Her low serum albumin","A low unmeasured cation level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Albumin accounts for most of the normal anion gap, so hypoalbuminemia lowers the gap and can hide added unmeasured anions. A normal gap does not exclude a high-gap acidosis and should not override clinical concern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player has cough, chest tightness and wheezing that start within 3 minutes after hard running, peak about 10 to 15 minutes later, and clear within an hour. She has no symptoms at rest or at night. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Moderate persistent asthma","Vocal fold dysfunction","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Catamenial asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is common in children and adolescents, begins during or within 3 minutes after exercise, peaks in 10 to 15 minutes and resolves by 60 minutes, exactly this player's pattern, with no symptoms at rest or at night. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis has worsening weakness and shallow, labored breathing over a day. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 57 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.27 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 57 is high and matches it (respiratory). Bicarbonate rose only about 1 per 10 mm Hg (17 above 40 gives about 26), so it is acute. Myasthenia gravis is a listed neuromuscular cause of acute respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 22, 26, 59"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a stable chronic respiratory acidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which serum electrolyte finding is expected?","choices":["High chloride","Low bicarbonate","Low chloride","An elevated anion gap"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride and retain bicarbonate, so hypochloremia accompanies the raised bicarbonate of 33. This is the renal compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man on home oxygen at 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier complains of a sore, dry nose and occasional bleeding. Which change addresses the cause?","choices":["Add humidity to the oxygen","Switch to a non-rebreather mask","Increase the flow to 6 L/min","Apply a petroleum lip and nose balm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He is on 5 L/min by nasal cannula with no humidifier, and has a sore, dry nose with bleeding. That is mucosal irritation. Humidity is used with cannula flow rates greater than 4 L/min, so adding humidity to the oxygen treats the cause. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 7, 9, 15"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with a panic attack is breathing fast, with perioral numbness and tingling hands. His roommate hands him a paper bag to breathe into. What is accurate education about the bag?","choices":["It is unnecessary and may lower his oxygen","It is needed to prevent tetany","It works by raising his bicarbonate","It safely corrects his carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Breathing into a paper bag is not necessary for hyperventilation: it fails to correct the carbon dioxide and may decrease oxygen, and any benefit is placebo. Hyperventilation is usually self-limited, and the cause is treated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which neuromuscular disorders are listed as causes of acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Pregnancy and hyperthyroidism","Muscular dystrophy and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Salicylates and nicotine","Myasthenia gravis and Guillain-Barre syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Myasthenia gravis and Guillain-Barre syndrome are the listed neuromuscular causes of acute respiratory acidosis. Muscular dystrophy and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis are the neuromuscular causes of the chronic form. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had 3 moderate exacerbations in the past year. Her blood eosinophil count is 110. She is starting maintenance therapy. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Daily oral corticosteroids","A single bronchodilator","Triple inhaled therapy","Dual long-acting bronchodilators"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Three moderate exacerbations make her high risk, group E, which starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. Her eosinophil count of 110 is below 300, so an inhaled corticosteroid, with its pneumonia risk, is not added. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man explored a cave with heavy bat droppings 2 weeks ago. He now has high fever, chills, myalgias, a nonproductive cough, chest pain and a headache. Which test should be ordered?","choices":["Chlamydia psittaci nucleic acid test","Serum cryptococcal antigen","Routine sputum bacterial culture","Urine Histoplasma antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exposure to bat droppings followed by fever, chills, myalgias, a dry cough, chest pain and headache within 3 to 17 days fits acute histoplasmosis. Testing uses the urine Histoplasma antigen, often with serum antigen and antibody tests. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 75, 76, 77"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with newly diagnosed pulmonary tuberculosis has a rapid molecular test showing rifampin resistance. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Standard four-drug regimen for 6 months","Add ethambutol for 2 more months","Rapid susceptibility testing and expert care","17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin resistance requires rapid susceptibility testing and expert management. Rifampin is one of the two drugs given through the whole 6-month standard regimen, so resistance to it takes him off the standard pathway. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 36"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 55% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 160 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1, and a body mass index of 27. Based on his BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index, what is his estimated 4-year survival?","choices":["18%","57%","67%","80%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 55% (50 to 64) = 1 + walk 160 meters (150 to 249) = 2 + dyspnea grade 1 = 0 + body mass index 27 (over 21) = 0, for a total of 3. Scores of 3 to 4 predict about 67% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has 3 weeks of progressive shortness of breath on exertion, a nonproductive cough, fever and weight loss. His respiratory rate is 28/min, and his lungs sound nearly normal. A chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral infiltrates spreading out from the perihilar regions. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection","Pneumocystis pneumonia","Pneumococcal lobar pneumonia","Acute histoplasmosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection is the leading risk, and Pneumocystis is the most common opportunistic infection in that group. Progressive exertional dyspnea, a nonproductive cough, fever, a near-normal lung examination and diffuse bilateral perihilar infiltrates are its pattern. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 58, 61, 62, 63, 70"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with cough and weight loss has a sputum culture that identifies Mycobacterium bovis. How is this organism classified?","choices":["A Mycobacterium avium complex member","A rapidly growing environmental species","A nontuberculous mycobacterium","A Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex member"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex causes tuberculosis; Mycobacterium tuberculosis is its principal human pathogen, and other members include Mycobacterium bovis and Mycobacterium africanum. Species identification matters because transmission and treatment differ from the nontuberculous mycobacteria. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has been intubated and mechanically ventilated for 3 days after a motor vehicle crash. He develops a new fever, purulent endotracheal secretions and a new infiltrate. How should this pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Health care-associated pneumonia","Hospital-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ventilator-associated pneumonia develops more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation, and he has been ventilated for 3 days. Colonized secretions inhaled through the tube cause it, and it carries higher mortality and a longer intensive care stay. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 48"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with Guillain-Barré syndrome has worsening weakness and shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the priority treatment for his acid-base disorder?","choices":["Restore adequate ventilation","Give sodium bicarbonate to raise the pH","Careful oxygen titration alone","Wait for renal compensation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A high carbon dioxide with a low pH and a barely raised bicarbonate is acute respiratory acidosis, and Guillain-Barré syndrome is a listed neuromuscular cause. Acute respiratory acidosis is treated by restoring ventilation while the cause is treated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which age group is named as a higher-progression-risk group after tuberculosis infection?","choices":["Older adulthood and retirement","Infancy and young childhood","Early and middle adulthood","Late adolescence and youth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infancy and young childhood are listed with the higher progression risks, alongside human immunodeficiency virus infection or major immunosuppression, tumor necrosis factor inhibitors, transplantation, prolonged corticosteroids and recent infection within 2 years. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 17"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with newly diagnosed persistent asthma starts using a peak flow meter at home. During his first weeks of readings while well, what is the main purpose of the readings?","choices":["Measuring his forced vital capacity","Replacing spirometry for diagnosis","Establishing his personal best baseline","Identifying his allergen triggers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home peak flow monitoring first establishes a baseline for the patient, his personal best, and then assesses control against it. His action-plan zones are set as percentages of that personal best, so the early readings are the foundation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 60, 88"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with diabetic ketoacidosis has a bicarbonate of 10 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and a carbon dioxide of 22 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, how is her respiratory compensation?","choices":["Appropriate","Too little: added respiratory acidosis","Too much: added respiratory alkalosis","It cannot be judged without the gap"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 10 + 8 = 23, plus or minus 2, so 21 to 25. Her measured 22 falls inside that range, so this is a simple, appropriately compensated metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with cirrhosis and hepatic failure also has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin and takes an antacid for heartburn. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of his conditions best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His hepatic failure","His antacid","Ketoacidosis from his diabetes","His metformin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 31 and a nearly normal bicarbonate is respiratory alkalosis. Hepatic failure is a listed cause of respiratory alkalosis; neither of his medicines lowers carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Alongside clinical judgment, which tool is preferred to support the admission decision in community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Pneumonia Severity Index","White blood cell count","Sputum culture result","Chest radiograph extent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Pneumonia Severity Index plus clinical judgment is preferred for admission decisions; the five-item confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure and age score complements it but does not replace clinical judgment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.55 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 34% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With the ratio below 0.70, the grade comes from the post-bronchodilator 1-second volume: 34% sits in the 30% to 49% band, grade 3, severe. Grade 4 would need less than 30%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man starting home oxygen asks why he must be so careful at home if oxygen is \"just air.\" Which property of oxygen underlies the safety teaching?","choices":["It turns into carbon monoxide","It is heavier than water","It is toxic to breathe for visitors","It is highly flammable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is highly flammable, which is why home oxygen teaching includes no smoking in the home, staying away from heat sources, avoiding petroleum products and posting signs. Oxygen is not just air, and fire is a listed hazard. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation takes a long-acting beta agonist, a long-acting antimuscarinic and an inhaled corticosteroid; he rarely uses albuterol. His atrial fibrillation episodes and palpitations have become more frequent since his regimen was started. Which component is most likely contributing?","choices":["His occasional albuterol","The inhaled corticosteroid","The long-acting antimuscarinic","The long-acting beta agonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta agonists produce sympathetic stimulation that can cause tachycardia and arrhythmias and can make atrial fibrillation worse; their cardiac effects are a listed concern in older adults. With rare albuterol use, the daily beta agonist is the likely driver. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 53"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a daytime resting oxygen saturation of 94% and normal saturation while walking, but overnight oximetry shows saturations of 86% at night only. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["No further evaluation","Oxygen during exertion","Evaluate for obstructive sleep apnea","Long-term oxygen over 15 hours a day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Desaturation to 88% or less at night only calls for evaluation for obstructive sleep apnea and screening for other causes, rather than long-term oxygen. His daytime resting and exertional saturations are normal. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is ready for discharge after his treatment is optimized. His resting saturation on room air is 87%, and he has no pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease) or high hematocrit. How often will his home oxygen be recertified?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His resting room-air saturation is 87%, at or below 88%, which is the Group I qualification (conditional Group II needs a comorbidity such as pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%). Group I patients are recertified every 12 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with no known tuberculosis risk factors has a tuberculin skin test for a school requirement. At 48 hours a trained reader measures 12 mm of transverse induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Indeterminate; repeat in 8 weeks","Positive; her cut-off is 5 mm","Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm","Positive; her cut-off is 10 mm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With no known tuberculosis risk factors, her cut-off is 15 mm, the highest of the three thresholds. 12 mm of induration is below 15 mm, so the result is negative for her, even though it would be positive for someone in the 5 mm or 10 mm groups. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22"},{"q":"A 20-year-old college student has had 10 days of gradual fever, a persistent cough and headache. Several students in her dormitory have the same illness, and a respiratory nucleic acid test is positive for Mycoplasma pneumoniae. She has taken amoxicillin for 3 days without improvement. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Extend the amoxicillin course","Switch to azithromycin","Switch to cefpodoxime","Add clavulanate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams are ineffective against Mycoplasma because it lacks a cell wall, so amoxicillin cannot work. Adults with Mycoplasma infection are treated with a macrolide such as azithromycin or with doxycycline, and a respiratory fluoroquinolone is an alternative. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with acute bronchitis was managed with supportive care 10 days ago and at first improved. She now returns with a new temperature of 102.2°F, shortness of breath and crackles at the left base. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassurance about cough length","Codeine cough syrup","Empiric bronchitis antibiotic","Chest radiograph for pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Worsening after initial improvement, fever and dyspnea are return precautions, and fever or focal findings prompt evaluation for pneumonia rather than empiric bronchitis antibiotics. A worsening cough means reconsidering the diagnosis, and suspected pneumonia is confirmed with chest radiography. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 20, 29"},{"q":"Above what fraction of inspired oxygen is an air-entrainment nebulizer not recommended?","choices":["0.30","0.50","0.80","1.0"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An air-entrainment nebulizer can deliver higher fractions of inspired oxygen, but use above 0.50 is not recommended. The recommended ceiling is 0.50, which corresponds to 50%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where in the United States is Histoplasma capsulatum mainly found?","choices":["The Middle East","Southeast and East Asia","Southwestern states","Central and eastern states"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the United States the fungus is mainly found in the central and eastern states, especially the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys, and it is acquired from soils with bird or bat droppings. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 75, 41"},{"q":"Sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106) and bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26). What is the calculated anion gap?","choices":["12","24","36","48"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate) = 140 - (104 + 12) = 140 - 116 = 24. That is above the normal 9 to 15, so this is an elevated anion gap, as expected with such a low bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is intubated for respiratory failure and admitted to the intensive care unit. He has no validated risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Levofloxacin monotherapy","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Vancomycin plus cefepime","Ceftriaxone as a single drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical ventilation makes this severe community-acquired pneumonia, treated with a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas coverage is added only for validated risks, which he lacks; cultures are obtained and therapy de-escalated later. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"How is exercise-induced bronchospasm best prevented?","choices":["A long-acting beta-2 agonist alone","Oral theophylline before games","Avoiding exercise entirely","Regular inhaled corticosteroid use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is treated with an inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist before exercise and is best prevented with regular inhaled corticosteroid use. It is common in children and adolescents, so this keeps teenagers in sport. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which process is listed as a contributor to airway remodeling and parenchymal destruction in this disease?","choices":["Excess surfactant production","Reversible smooth muscle spasm alone","Immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell activation","Protease-antiprotease imbalance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Airway remodeling and parenchymal destruction are driven by chronic inflammation, oxidative stress and protease-antiprotease imbalance. That imbalance is also why a lack of alpha-1 antitrypsin, a protease inhibitor, destroys the lung. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman in diabetic ketoacidosis has Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does her breathing pattern accomplish?","choices":["It is a primary respiratory alkalosis","It retains carbon dioxide to raise her pH","It signals impending respiratory failure","It lowers carbon dioxide to offset the acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis is compensated by increased ventilation to decrease carbon dioxide; in severe cases hyperventilation is profound, as Kussmaul respirations (increased tidal volume and tachypnea) in diabetic ketoacidosis. Her carbon dioxide of 20 shows it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with uncomplicated influenza is going home with supportive care. Which development should prompt him to return urgently?","choices":["Improvement followed by worsening","A cough lasting past one week","A temperature of 100°F on day 2","Fatigue that lingers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Return precautions for influenza are shortness of breath, chest pain, dehydration, confusion, or improvement followed by worsening. A relapse after improving can signal a secondary bacterial pneumonia, a known complication of influenza. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with asthma wakes with cough and mild wheeze. Her peak flow is 70% of her personal best, placing her in the yellow zone of her written action plan. What does the plan direct her to do first?","choices":["Add quick-relief medicine and keep her controller","Stop her controller until she feels better","Start an oral steroid right away","Go to the hospital now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the yellow zone, 50% to 79% of personal best, asthma is getting worse: she adds quick-relief medicine (a short-acting beta-2 agonist) and keeps taking her green-zone controller. An oral steroid is added only if she does not return to green after an hour. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man is diagnosed with latent tuberculosis infection after a normal clinical evaluation. He lives with his newborn daughter and asks whether he could pass the infection to her. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["He is contagious until his treatment ends","He is contagious when he coughs or sings","Latent infection does not spread to others","He can spread it through close household contact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. People with latent tuberculosis infection are infected but asymptomatic, are not infectious and cannot spread the disease; latent infection does not cause transmission. Spread comes from infectious aerosols released by pulmonary or laryngeal disease during coughing, speaking or singing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 13"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with Crohn disease has had profuse watery diarrhea for five days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 112 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.31 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 15 is low and matches it: metabolic. Gap = 137 - (112 + 15) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride, so it is a normal-gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis. Bicarbonate loss in diarrhea fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 47, 52, 56"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection starts trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis pneumonia. Her arterial oxygen pressure on room air is 61 mm Hg. Which therapy should be added?","choices":["A systemic corticosteroid","No adjunct is needed","An inhaled corticosteroid","Intravenous pentamidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adjunctive corticosteroids are indicated in Pneumocystis pneumonia linked to human immunodeficiency virus when the room-air arterial oxygen pressure is below 70 mm Hg, and hers is 61. Prednisone is started as early as possible, ideally within 72 hours of starting treatment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with asthma and nasal polyps developed wheezing, facial flushing and a runny nose within an hour of taking aspirin last year. She now has painful knee osteoarthritis and asks for an anti-inflammatory pain reliever. Which drug is the most appropriate choice?","choices":["Diclofenac","Celecoxib","Naproxen","Indomethacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her wheeze, flushing and rhinorrhea after aspirin mark aspirin-sensitive asthma, so non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs must be avoided. Selective agents such as celecoxib or meloxicam may be used, which treats her knee pain without the cross-reaction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman completes the 8-item symptom assessment test, each item scored 0 to 5: cough 2, phlegm 1, chest tightness 1, breathlessness on a hill or flight of stairs 3, limitation of home activities 2, confidence leaving home 1, sleep 1, and energy 2. What is her total score?","choices":["11","12","13","14"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cough 2 + phlegm 1 + chest tightness 1 + breathlessness on stairs 3 + home activities 2 + confidence leaving home 1 + sleep 1 + energy 2 = 13. A total of 10 or more counts as more symptoms when assigning her group. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Between exacerbations, the physical examination of a patient with asthma is often what?","choices":["Normal","Marked by a barrel chest","Marked by fixed wheezing","Marked by digital clubbing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The examination may be normal between exacerbations, which is why an objective lung function measurement is needed for the diagnosis. Extrinsic asthma may still show nasal mucosal swelling, increased secretions, eczema or nasal polyps. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on home oxygen restores old cars in his garage and rubs grease off his hands with an oil-based cleanser while wearing his cannula. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Use a Venturi mask when working","Turn the flow up while working","Wear a simple mask in the garage","Avoid petroleum-based oils and grease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to avoid petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease. He is wearing his cannula while rubbing grease off with an oil-based cleanser, so he should avoid those petroleum oils and grease. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 7, 13, 16"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has had stable, well-controlled asthma for 2 years. How often should his spirometry be repeated to monitor progression?","choices":["Every month","Every 3 months","Every year","Every 5 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry is best done before and after a short-acting bronchodilator and is repeated annually to monitor progression. For this man with stable, controlled disease, a yearly test tracks his lung function between routine follow-up visits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with moderate persistent asthma comes in for her yearly influenza vaccine, and her mother asks whether she can have the nasal spray form instead of a shot. Which vaccine form calls for caution in her?","choices":["Any influenza vaccine this year","The intranasal influenza vaccine","Every vaccine during asthma treatment","The pneumonia vaccine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Annual influenza vaccination is recommended because vaccine-preventable illness can trigger asthma exacerbations, but intranasal influenza vaccines call for caution, since they can be associated with exacerbation in younger patients such as this 14-year-old. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a lung abscess has had two episodes of Clostridioides difficile colitis in the past year. Which part of a lung abscess plan should be avoided for her?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Clindamycin","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Several weeks of treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is reserved for selected alternatives because of Clostridioides difficile risk, and she has had two episodes of that colitis. Her abscess is treated with a beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor instead, intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam followed by oral amoxicillin-clavulanate. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Smoking-related emphysema typically follows which pattern?","choices":["Centrilobular, upper lobes","Diffuse fibrosis at the bases","Panlobular, lower lobes","Bronchial wall thickening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema from smoking is centrilobular and predominantly in the upper lobes. The panlobular, lower-lobe pattern belongs to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, which is why basilar emphysema prompts testing for it. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is on a Venturi mask set at 28%. His saturation is still below his target, and he is comfortable and alert. Which adjustment raises his fraction precisely?","choices":["Change to a higher-fraction port","Switch to a nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Add a reservoir bag to the mask","Switch to a non-rebreather at 15 L/min"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A Venturi mask changes its fraction through different ports (24-50% when flow is adjusted from 4-10 L/min). He is stable and comfortable at 28% but below his target, so the precise adjustment is a higher-fraction port. It keeps the fraction fixed. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia is alert and oriented. Her blood urea nitrogen is 17 mg/dL, respiratory rate 34/min and blood pressure 132/56 mm Hg. What is her CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 0 + urea 0 (17 is not above 20) + respiratory rate 1 (34 is 30 or more) + blood pressure 1 (a diastolic of 56 is 60 or lower, even with a normal systolic) + age 1 (81) = 3. That is high severity, with a risk of death of 15 to 40%: hospital and urgent senior review. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man gets hives whenever he eats eggs and asks whether he can receive this season's influenza vaccine. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine. What is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Skip influenza vaccination","Vaccinate under specialist supervision","Routine vaccination and safety steps","An egg-free product"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Egg allergy does not require an egg-free product or precautions beyond routine vaccine safety measures. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine, so he receives an age- and health-appropriate vaccine this season like any other adult. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 6"}]};
